{"duration": 55.679, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "Robert Hooke cradled his newly\u2011made microscope like a newborn.", "start": 0.08, "end": 3.478}, {"beat": 0, "text": "He had spent months grinding glass into the tiniest balls.", "start": 3.698, "end": 6.654}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Most of his peers treated microscopes as party tricks.", "start": 7.074, "end": 10.037}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Few believed a glass bead could reveal nature\u2019s secrets.", "start": 10.257, "end": 13.241}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Hooke fused threads of spun glass into perfect spheres.", "start": 13.661, "end": 16.363}, {"beat": 2, "text": "He mounted those spheres in a leather\u2011and\u2011gold\u2011tooled tube he built himself.", "start": 16.583, "end": 20.439}, {"beat": 3, "text": "In 1665 he published *Micrographia*, flooding Europe with his drawings.", "start": 20.86, "end": 26.703}, {"beat": 3, "text": "He described the first fungus ever seen and coined the word \u201ccell\u201d.", "start": 26.923, "end": 30.242}, {"beat": 4, "text": "You see, it\u2019s like keeping a single pearl safe in a velvet pouch.", "start": 30.662, "end": 33.871}, {"beat": 4, "text": "One slip and the pearl is shattered forever.", "start": 34.091, "end": 36.098}, {"beat": 5, "text": "The real reason Hooke wrapped his microscope was simple:", "start": 36.518, "end": 39.211}, {"beat": 5, "text": "the glass balls were so tiny that a tiny shock could crack them.", "start": 39.431, "end": 42.744}, {"beat": 5, "text": "By shielding the whole instrument, he kept his lenses from breaking.", "start": 42.964, "end": 46.274}, {"beat": 6, "text": "The very same year the Great Fire swept London, Hooke helped redraw the city\u2019s map.", "start": 46.694, "end": 51.05}, {"beat": 6, "text": "The man who saved his lenses also helped rebuild a capital.", "start": 51.27, "end": 54.559}], "words": [{"w": "Robert", "start": 0.149, "end": 0.474, "line": 0, "beat": 0}, {"w": "Hooke", "start": 0.524, "e